  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Of 100 senior school students, 40 are taking chemistry, 40 are taking biology and 30 are taking physics. Moreover, 24 are taking exactly two of these subjects, and 3 are taking all three subjects. A student is randomly chosen. Find the probability that the student is taking none of these subjects.\n\nHint: Draw a Venn diagram.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p17-q11-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\frac{1}{5}",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p17-q11-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Correct for repeated counting: 80 students take at least one subject, so 20 take none.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "In a three-set Venn diagram, the central region contains 3 students and the three exactly-two regions contain 24 students altogether. The sum $40+40+30$ counts each exactly-two student twice and each all-three student three times.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}n(\\text{at least one})&=40+40+30-24-2\\times3\\\\n(\\text{at least one})&=80\\\\n(\\text{none})&=100-80\\\\n(\\text{none})&=20\\\\\\Pr(\\text{none})&=\\frac{20}{100}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{none})&=\\frac15\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
